import Foundation

enum Prompts {
    static let label = """
    You describe a screenshot of a web page or app screen for a design-scoring system. The text was already read by OCR: you get the image plus a list "id|x,y,w,h|text" (pixels of the image). Do NOT invent, change or add text boxes; label the given ids. Reply with ONE JSON object only, no prose. Pick from the listed words; never invent a number.
    {"page_name":"page name, 3 words at most, plain words only, no separators or punctuation like · | - : /, e.g. \"TypeSafe API Keys\", \"Stripe Home\" or \"Profile\". Empty string if unsure",
     "page_type":"landing|dashboard|store|article|form|other",
     "screen_kind":"marketing|app|modal|fragment|document",
     "tone":"plain_confident|hedging|stiff|slangy|no_copy",
     "visual_description":"2 sentences about ONLY how it looks (layout, density, color, imagery). No copy quotes.",
     "primary_buttons":"none|one|several",
     "blocks":"few|many","fonts":"one_or_two|three_plus","main_text_alignment":"left|center|mixed|scattered",
     "nav_position":"top|left|none|other","logo_position":"top-left|top-center|other|none","top_right_control":false,
     "panels":"few|many","columns":"up_to_three|four_plus",
     "has_dated_cues":false,"has_overlay":false,"has_competing_promo":false,"has_proof":false,"real_imagery":false,
     "first_fixation":"headline|logo|image|ad|button|other","images_ok":true,"text_on_busy_bg":false,
     "grouping_clear":true,"links_look_clickable":true,"nav_quieter_than_headline":true,"emphasis":"few|many",
     "has_layout_error":false,"has_inconsistent_style":false,"has_unusual_pattern":false,"follows_page_type_pattern":true,
     "business_model":"ecommerce|saas|information|agency|other","business_model_confidence":"high|medium|low",
     "location":"homepage|landing|elsewhere|inside|not_a_page","page_purpose":"3 words at most naming what this page is for, e.g. \"API keys\", \"Pricing\", \"Cart\". Empty string if unsure",
     "corner_style":"rounded|sharp|mixed","heaviest_weight":"regular|medium|bold|black",
     "roles":{"heading":[],"subhead":[],"nav":[],"button":[],"link":[],"logo":[],"demo":[],"legal":[],"primary":[]}}
    Write every field above BEFORE "roles"; roles come last so a long list can never cut the fields off.
    roles lists box ids. heading: the main headlines and section titles. subhead: the line directly under a headline. nav: top or side navigation items. button: text inside a button or call-to-action. link: other clickable text (not nav, not button). logo: the site's own brand name AND every customer/partner logo word in a logo strip (e.g. amazon, Google, NVIDIA). demo: ALL text inside a product mockup, app window, chart, code or fake data, including its sidebar, tabs, labels and counters (not page copy). legal: footer legal text. primary: the id of the single strongest filled call-to-action button (empty if none). Anything not listed is plain body copy; do not list body copy. Keep each list short: only the boxes that clearly fit. An id sits in at most one list (primary is the exception).
    business_model: ecommerce = the page sells goods (cart, prices with Add to cart, product grid); saas = software people sign up for or use (free trial, pricing tiers, app sidebar, settings, tables of their own objects); information = sells knowledge or content (courses, newsletters, media, coaching: modules, Enroll, Subscribe, author photo, articles); agency = sells services (case studies, portfolio, Book a call, client logos); other = none of these or cannot tell. If two look equally likely use other and low confidence. Judge only what is visible; ignore text inside product mockups. location: homepage = a site's front page with full navigation and several sections; landing = one offer with one repeated call to action and little navigation; elsewhere = any other public page (pricing, blog, about, docs, product or category page); inside = a screen of the product itself behind login (dashboard, settings, editor, account, orders, cart or checkout); not_a_page = ONLY an image that is clearly not a web or app screen: a terminal, diagram, slide, chart, code editor or a crop with no page chrome. A full web page with a header, menus, prices, banners or product photos is NEVER not_a_page, even when it is chaotic. Any recognizable website or app UI, however messy or cluttered, gets a real location (a store front page is homepage). A marketing header with Log in / Sign up means outside the product; an app sidebar or the user's own data means inside.
    screen_kind: marketing = landing, pricing, store or product page; app = a screen of an app or tool (settings, dashboard, list, editor, form); modal = a dialog, sheet or popover; fragment = a crop of part of a screen (a few cards, a panel, a diagram) with no page chrome; never use fragment for a full browser window or full web page, however cluttered or ugly; document = an article, email or text document. tone judges only the wording: plain_confident = direct, specific, human but professional; hedging = unsure, softened; stiff = corporate or buzzword-heavy; slangy = too casual; no_copy = almost no readable wording. follows_page_type_pattern is judged against the usual layout of its OWN screen_kind (an app screen is not expected to have a hero or proof; a fragment is judged on alignment and spacing only). Word scales: blocks (separate groups of text or images): few = 7 or fewer, many = 8 or more. panels (cards or boxed areas): few = 10 or fewer, many = 11 or more. emphasis (items shouted by bold, color or size near the top): few = 3 or fewer, many = 4 or more. has_dated_cues is true for table borders, bevelled buttons, clip-art, blinking text, or 3+ bright text colors. has_inconsistent_style is true when same-kind items (buttons, headings) differ in shape, style or font. real_imagery is true only when the page shows a real product UI screenshot (app window, dashboard, editor) or a photo of a real person, even if partly cropped. Photos of products for sale, toys, vehicles or cartoon drawings are NOT real_imagery. has_competing_promo is true for ads and unrelated third-party promos only; the site's own announcement bar or "New" link is NOT one. has_overlay is true for pop-ups, chat widgets, cookie bars, ads covering content. Ignore sample text inside product mockups.
    """

    static let c6 = """
    You judge a web page screenshot. Question: does the main headline say what the product is or does, in plain words (no slogan or metaphor)? Reply with ONE JSON object only: {\"C6\":true} or {\"C6\":false}. No prose, no explanation: start your reply with {.
    """

    static let write = """
    Write feedback for ONE screen design check. For each key give [why, fix, problem]. why and fix: ONE plain sentence each, short common words, no jargon, no hedging words. When the score is 100 or nothing needs fixing, return "" (empty string) as fix. Each must fit in 3 short lines: aim for 120 characters or fewer, and always end the sentence. Name the exact element (e.g. the “Sign in” link, the top nav). Quote at most 4 words. fix starts with a verb. problem is 1 when why names a problem, else 0. Use only the given facts. Keys: order balance grouping contrast familiar ease clarity trust reading valence intensity. For valence and intensity, why names what ON THE PAGE drives it, using feeling_facts (elements, colors, words), e.g. "Dark navy and muted grey keep it calm; the copy is neutral."; fix is "" and problem 0. For reading, why names the hardest words and the longest sentence from reading_facts (quote at most 4 words), and fix is "" when grade is 9 or lower. Confident, no hedging, one sentence each. Reply ONE compact JSON object: {"first":["why","fix",0],...}
    """
}
